/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE SOLUTES WERE DIVIDED BY 20 (2026-07-27), and the reason is that the
  first version put the case outside the domain of the model it declares.

  Molality follows from the water, not from the kmol/h: 60 kmol/h of water is
  1081 kg/h, so the original 6 kmol/h of ammonia was 5.55 mol/kg and the
  4 kmol/h of CO2 was 3.70 mol/kg.  With half the ammonia protonated and
  matched by bicarbonate that is an ionic strength near 2.8 mol/kg.

  The thermoPhysPropDict declares DAVIES, whose trust range is ~0.5 mol/kg
  and which now warns above it.  The case was running at five times its own
  warning threshold.  That is not a precision detail: at I ~ 3 the Davies
  form is not approximating badly, it is outside the regime where it means
  anything, and the activity coefficients -- and with them the pH, every
  saturation index and the whole partition -- would be fiction.

  A concentrated brine is a legitimate system.  It is just a PITZER system,
  and this case is about the second liquid phase, not the multi-salt engine.

  MEASURED at the new loading (speciate on the aqueous sub-system, 308.15 K):

      I  = 0.277 mol/kg          -- inside the Davies domain, no advisory
      pH = 6.92                  -- the ammonia/carbonate buffer holding
      calcite saturates at       -- Ca ~ 3.3e-4 mol/kg

  water, benzene and ethanol are UNCHANGED: diluting them would have shrunk
  the organic phase, and the two liquids are what this case exists to drive.

  CaCO3 is now 0.002 kmol/h against a saturation of ~0.00035 kmol/h, so
  roughly one sixth of it dissolves and the rest stays as suspended solid.
  The original 0.5 kmol/h was ~1400 times the saturation: the comment said
  "may dissolve" while 99.9 % of it could only sit there as ballast, and the
  coupling between the two carbonate solids -- the whole point of the case --
  was never exercised because the calcium never reached solution in a
  quantity that could matter.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
componentMolarFlows
{
    water          60.0    kmol/h;
    benzene        20.0    kmol/h;
    ethanol         8.0    kmol/h;
    N2              5.0    kmol/h;    // inert -- guarantees a vapour phase
    CO2             0.2    kmol/h;
    NH3             0.3    kmol/h;
    H2S             0.05   kmol/h;
    aceticAcid      0.1    kmol/h;
    CaCO3           0.002  kmol/h;    // ~6x saturation: part dissolves,
                                      //   the rest stays suspended solid
}
T               308.15 K;
P               2.0 atm;
